# Intro

This example is a non cosmological hydro-only homogeneous box with
gas. A gas particle at the center of the box is initialized with non null
metallicity, while the other particles are metal-free. *This example is used to
test _constant istotropic_ metal diffusion with the GEAR diffusion chemistry
models.*

The default level is 5 (N_particle = 2^(3*level)). It is quick. If you want to
try the example with higher levels, we recommend using HPC facilities.

# Configure

To run this example with GEAR model,

``` ./configure --with-chemistry=X --with-cooling=grackle_0 --with-stars=GEAR
--with-star-formation=GEAR --with-feedback=GEAR --with-kernel=wendland-C2
--with-grackle=path/to/grackle ```

with - X=`GEAR_10` for smoothed metallicity scheme, - X=`GEAR-FVPM-DIFFUSION`
for the parabolic diffusion scheme, - X=`GEAR-FVPM-HYPERBOLIC-DIFFUSION_10` for
the hyperbolic diffusion scheme.

and then

make -j

Note that you can also use the Gizmo MFM scheme to test diffusion by adding
`--with-hydro=gizmo-mfm --with-riemann-solver=hllc` to the configuration step.

# ICs

The run.sh script calls `makeIC.py' script with default values. You can
experiment by changing the ICs. Run `python3 makeIC.py --help` to get the list
of parameters.

# Run

Type run.sh and let's go!

You can provide parameters to the running scripts, have a look at them.

# Debugging notes

You can track the metal mass injected by feedback and diffused
by the chemitry module by adding `CFLAGS="-DSWIFT_CHEMISTRY_DEBUG_CHECKS"
./configure [other parameters]`. This will also output the total diffused metal
mass by each particles over the simulation. If you sum these values, you can
track metal mass conservation. Notice that star formation removes metal.

# Caveats: interpreting this test for hyperbolic diffusion

This test is deliberately harsh: a near-delta metal seed in the wave-dominated
regime (large `tau`), where the exact solution is a thin expanding shell rather
than a smooth blob (at `tau=10`, the front carries over 99% of the metal mass
by `t=5`). No particle scheme at these resolutions can represent a shell
thinner than the interparticle spacing, so front smearing and apparent
super-causal leakage are expected, growing with `tau`. There is also a hard
ceiling here: the exact front carries a signed negative-density lobe, so a
positivity-preserving scheme like this one can never simultaneously match
causality and the exact second moment.  Judge spread/front behaviour against
that ceiling, not the signed exact curve alone.

When comparing to `metal_profile.py`'s exact reference curves, pass `--epsilon`
matching the seed radius the run was generated with (`0.02` for older archived
runs, `0.04` for the current default), or the curve will be offset. At level 5
use `epsilon >= 0.04` — below the interparticle spacing (about 0.023) only a
single particle gets seeded, a maximally adversarial point source dominated by
discreteness noise. The periodic box also saturates quickly: once `c_hyp * t +
epsilon` exceeds the half-extent (about 0.37 kpc), wrapped metal contaminates
the diagnostics, so restrict quantitative comparisons to early times,
especially for `tau <= 1`.

In production, hyperbolic diffusion runs in the smooth-gradient regime (see
`MetalDiffusionDiscontinuity`), where it is accurate and causal. This test's
extreme setup is closest to discrete supernova injection, which can resemble a
delta or thin Gaussian ball of metal — expect similar behaviour for a few
relaxation times after such an injection.
